[1m[38;2;236;230;217mthe causeway into Millholm[0m[39m[49m · [38;2;165;159;148m25 Haysel, morning · humid[39m
[38;2;110;106;98m───────────────────────────────────────────────────────[39m
Someone is cutting reed in the ditch beside the causeway, up to the knees in it. They straighten to
watch you go by.
A cart stands canted at the side of the road, one wheel down in the ditch to the hub. The horse is
blowing in the shafts, and a sack of meal has burst on the bed. The wagoner is up to his knees in
the ditch with his shoulder to the wheel. He looks round at the sound of you and does not ask. He
has asked enough people today.
[1mAn aim: Your father went out by the road between Mistshaw and Millholm with the cart[0m[39m[49m · a to follow

[2m› To the camp at Millholm[0m[39m[49m

[1m[38;2;236;230;217mthe camp at Millholm[0m[39m[49m
[38;2;110;106;98m────────────────────[39m
Millholm is half houses and half wagons with their wheels taken off, painted saffron and rose and a
deep blue. Cook-smoke drifts between them, smelling of cumin and charred onion.
Your brother is sitting on a rail with your father’s old spear across his knees, watching the road.
He is taller by a head than when you went away.
The grey dog barks twice, for form’s sake, then comes to see who you are.

